  <section>
    <h1>Year-End Home Buying Tax Benefits for Military Families</h1>
    <p class="lead">Home ownership offers significant tax advantages. Military families purchasing in late 2026 should understand available deductions and plan strategically for maximum tax benefit.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Mortgage Interest Deduction</h2>
    <p>The most valuable homeownership tax benefit is the mortgage interest deduction. Here's what you need to know:</p>
    <p><strong>What You Can Deduct:</strong> Interest paid on mortgages up to $750,000 (married filing jointly) or $375,000 (single) is deductible from your federal taxable income. Most San Antonio homeowners won't exceed these limits.</p>
    <p><strong>Your First Year:</strong> If you purchase in November or December 2026, you'll deduct mortgage interest paid in those two months on your 2026 tax return. This is typically modest since you've just started the mortgage, but it still provides some benefit.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If you purchase a $450,000 home on November 15, 2026 at 6.5% interest, your November-December interest payment is approximately $2,000. This is deductible from your 2026 income, potentially saving you $500-700 in federal taxes.</p>
    <p><strong>2027 and Beyond:</strong> The real benefit hits in 2027 and subsequent years. A full year of mortgage interest deductions at $450,000 loan amount and 6.5% rate equals approximately $29,250 annually—a substantial deduction.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Property Tax Deduction</h2>
    <p>Texas property taxes are deductible from your federal taxes (within limits):</p>
    <p><strong>The SALT Cap:</strong> State and Local Tax (SALT) deductions are capped at $10,000 annually. This includes state income tax (Texas has none, which is actually an advantage), property taxes, and local taxes combined.</p>
    <p><strong>For San Antonio Homeowners:</strong> Texas property tax rates run 1.6-1.9% of home value. On a $450,000 home, annual property taxes are approximately $7,200-8,550. Since Texas has no income tax, your entire property tax bill is deductible (within the $10,000 SALT cap).</p>
    <p><strong>Year-End Purchase Timing:</strong> If you purchase in November or December, you'll pay property taxes for November-December prorated at closing. This is deductible in 2026, though the amount is modest (approximately $600-700 for 2 months).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How Tax Benefits Stack: Real Example</h2>
    <p>Let's look at concrete numbers for a military family purchasing a $450,000 home in November 2026:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mortgage Interest (Nov-Dec 2026):</strong> ~$2,000</li>
      <li><strong>Property Tax (prorated):</strong> ~$700</li>
      <li><strong>Total 2026 Deductions:</strong> ~$2,700</li>
      <li><strong>Tax Savings (at 24% tax bracket):</strong> ~$650</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In 2027 with a full year of homeownership:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mortgage Interest (12 months):</strong> ~$29,250</li>
      <li><strong>Property Tax (full year):</strong> ~$8,100</li>
      <li><strong>Total 2027 Deductions:</strong> ~$37,350</li>
      <li><strong>Tax Savings (at 24% tax bracket):</strong> ~$8,964</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These tax savings make homeownership significantly more affordable than renting. Over 30 years, the accumulated tax benefit of homeownership is substantial.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing</h2>
    <p>To benefit from mortgage and property tax deductions, you must itemize deductions rather than take the standard deduction:</p>
    <p><strong>2026 Standard Deduction:</strong> $13,850 (single) or $27,700 (married filing jointly)</p>
    <p><strong>When Itemizing Makes Sense:</strong> If your mortgage interest plus property taxes plus other itemized deductions (charitable contributions, medical expenses, etc.) exceed the standard deduction, itemizing saves you money.</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> A married military couple with $29,250 mortgage interest and $8,100 property tax ($37,350 total) should itemize rather than take the $27,700 standard deduction—saving them taxes on $9,650 additional income.</p>
    <p>Most homeowners with mortgages benefit from itemizing. Consult a tax professional to confirm your situation, but expect homeownership tax advantages.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Points and Closing Costs</h2>
    <p>Mortgage points (prepaid interest) can sometimes be deductible, but rules are complex:</p>
    <p><strong>Points on Primary Residence:</strong> Points paid to secure a mortgage are generally deductible over the life of the loan (for a 30-year mortgage, deduct 1/30 of your points each year).</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> If you pay $4,500 in points on a 30-year mortgage, you can deduct $150 per year ($4,500 / 30), plus deduct it all immediately if you pay off the mortgage early.</p>
    <p><strong>Other Closing Costs:</strong> Most closing costs (appraisal, title insurance, inspections, etc.) are not deductible. Only interest-related costs may be deductible.</p>
    <p>Discuss point deductibility with a tax professional or CPA. The tax benefit sometimes justifies paying points to lower your interest rate.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Military-Specific Tax Advantages</h2>
    <p>Beyond standard homeownership deductions, military families enjoy specific tax considerations:</p>
    <p><strong>VA Loan Benefits:</strong> No PMI on VA loans saves you thousands in non-deductible insurance premiums compared to conventional mortgages. This isn't a direct tax deduction, but it's a real financial advantage.</p>
    <p><strong>Military BAH Deduction (If Self-Employed):</strong> If you're a military family with a self-employed spouse, BAH may be deductible as a business expense. This is complex and requires professional guidance—consult a CPA familiar with military finances.</p>
    <p><strong>PCS Move Deductions (If Employer-Paid):</strong> While personal moving expenses aren't deductible, if your military service or employer reimburses moving costs, those reimbursements are generally tax-free.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Capital Gains and Future Home Sales</h2>
    <p>Military families relocate frequently. When you eventually sell your San Antonio home, you'll benefit from capital gains exclusion:</p>
    <p><strong>Exclusion Amount:</strong> If you've owned and lived in your primary residence for 2 of the last 5 years, you can exclude $250,000 of gains from taxes (married couples can exclude $500,000).</p>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> You purchase a home for $450,000 in November 2026, improve it, and sell it for $520,000 in 2029 (you've owned it 2+ years). Your $70,000 gain is completely excluded from taxes due to the primary residence exclusion.</p>
    <p>This is a powerful advantage of homeownership that renters don't enjoy. Property appreciation becomes your wealth-building tool with favorable tax treatment.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Year-End Home Buying Strategy</h2>
    <p>From a tax perspective, November or December purchases make sense for military families because:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Immediate Deductions:</strong> You get mortgage interest and property tax deductions on your 2026 return, even though you only owned the home 1-2 months.</li>
      <li><strong>Full Year in 2027:</strong> You'll have a full 12 months of deductions in 2027, maximizing your first full year of homeownership tax benefits.</li>
      <li><strong>PCS Timeline Alignment:</strong> Year-end purchases often align with military relocation timelines, making the purchase strategically logical for both real estate and military reasons.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Planning Beyond Tax Benefits</h2>
    <p>Tax benefits are valuable but shouldn't be your primary home-buying motivation. Consider homeownership strategically:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Long-Term Investment:</strong> Homeownership builds wealth through forced savings (mortgage payment) and potential appreciation. Tax benefits enhance this—they don't drive the decision.</li>
      <li><strong>Stability:</strong> For military families, homeownership provides stability and roots in a community. This quality-of-life benefit often outweighs financial calculations.</li>
      <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Military families should understand rental vs. ownership decisions. If your next PCS is uncertain, renting provides flexibility. If you're building long-term roots in San Antonio, ownership makes sense.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

